# Design trade-off comparisons for Step 17 §2 ("go deeper: compare two designs for the hardest area")
DESIGN_TRADEOFFS = [
    ('Area 1: Recruiter Candidate Search Visibility + Consent Enforcement under RLS (AP-04 / AP-05, FND-09)',
     'Design A — Per-row helper function call in RLS policy (`USING (app_candidate_visible(id, app_user_id, profile_visibility_id, deleted_at))` with correlated `EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM consent_current ...)`). Simple 1-line policy, zero duplication between point checks and table scans.',
     'Design B — Inline RLS predicate with scalar `(SELECT ...)` initPlan array helpers (`NOT id = ANY ((SELECT app_candidates_blocking_viewer())::bigint[]) AND NOT app_user_id = ANY ((SELECT app_users_without_recruiter_consent())::bigint[])`) plus parity test in `t03_security.sql`.',
     'Chosen: **Design B**. PostgreSQL cannot inline a `SECURITY DEFINER` function containing subqueries into the outer plan. At 10k profiles, Design A executes viewer role/block/consent lookups 10,000 times per search (measured **3,700 ms**). Design B evaluates the `(SELECT fn())` initPlans **once per query** and probes the index/arrays (measured **3.4 ms** at 10k profiles — a **1,088× speedup**). Parity test `t03` guarantees `app_candidate_visible()` and `candidate_profile_read` never drift.'),
    ('Area 2: High-Concurrency Quota Enforcement — Wallet Balance, Licence Seats, Device Limits & Job Positions (FR-BIL-03, FR-ORG-05, FR-IAM-05, FR-JOB-08)',
     'Design A — `SERIALIZABLE` transaction isolation level or `AFTER INSERT` deferred constraint triggers (`SUM(credits) >= 0`, `COUNT(*) <= max_seats`) without parent row locks.',
     'Design B — `READ COMMITTED` with explicit parent-entity `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` row locks (`wallet` row for credits, `organisation` row for seats, `app_user` row for devices, `job_requirement` row for positions) inside single-writer functions/triggers + cached counters (`balance_credits`, `positions_filled`) verified by `CHECK` constraints.',
     'Chosen: **Design B**. Under `READ COMMITTED`, two concurrent transactions do not see each other\'s uncommitted inserts, so a trigger counting rows without a parent lock allows both to pass (write skew). `SERIALIZABLE` fixes this by aborting racing transactions with `40001 serialization_failure`, forcing application retry loops on every hot wallet or job. Design B serialises only the contenders for the *same* wallet/org/user/job in a deterministic queue (`t04_concurrency.sql` proves 8 parallel debits on 3 credits → 3 succeed, 5 cleanly refused with `insufficient credits`, 0 serialization aborts).'),
    ('Area 3: State Machine Enforcement for Applications & Job Requirements (Step 08, FND-01, FND-02)',
     'Design A — Hardcoded `CASE / IF` transition branches inside PL/pgSQL triggers or API service code.',
     'Design B — Data-driven configuration tables (`application_status_transition`, `job_status_transition`) with foreign keys to lookup tables, enforced by a generic `EXISTS` check inside the table guard trigger.',
     'Chosen: **Design B**. Storing allowed moves as rows makes the state machine inspectable by SQL (`t05` R-05 verifies every history row against `application_status_transition` in one join), self-documenting in the ER model, and extensible by a data migration without rewriting trigger control flow.'),
]

